{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 2564 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "ae0ab4ed6e7180613d861b12c6d3895d1b956ee7233f335bad06e7e11b2e18eb" :edb/db-before-t 2563 :edb/db-after-t 2564 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535876 50 #inst "2026-10-09T09:36:01.084Z" 13194139535876 true] [17592186166832 1020 #edb/ref 17592186371888 13194139535876 true] [17592186166832 1020 #edb/ref 17592186371895 13194139535876 true] [17592186166833 1020 #edb/ref 17592186371867 13194139535876 true] [17592186166833 1020 #edb/ref 17592186371874 13194139535876 true] [17592186166833 1020 #edb/ref 17592186371881 13194139535876 true] [17592186371867 1017 #edb/ref 17592186371868 13194139535876 true] [17592186371867 1089 "q-330297" 13194139535876 true] [17592186371867 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535876 true] [17592186371867 1100 "Let ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ satisfy the homogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=A\\text{ }x(t)$ and ${x}_{p}(t)$ satisfy the inhomogeneous system\n${x}^{′}(t)=A\\text{ }x(t)+f(t).$\nWhich of the following is **always** a solution of the same inhomogeneous system?\n\n- $2\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)$\n- $-5\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{p}(t)$\n- $3\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)-2\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)+3\\cdot {x}_{p}(t)$" 13194139535876 true] [17592186371867 1113 #uuid "a4494cb8-4770-534e-9ebf-f865a2fdba57" 13194139535876 true] [17592186371867 1145 "Recall that if ${x}_{h}(t)$ satisfies a homogeneous linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ and ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution to an inhomogeneous linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t),$ then ${x}_{h}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)$ is also a solution to the same inhomogeneous system.\n\nAdditonally, the *linearity principle* for systems of linear ordinary differential equations (ODEs) states that if ${x}_{1}(t),$ ${x}_{2}(t),…,{x}_{k}(t)$ are solutions to a homogeneous linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ then any linear combination \n\n$$\n{c}_{1}{x}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{x}_{2}(t)+⋯+{c}_{k}{x}_{k}(t)\n$$\n\nwith ${c}_{1},{c}_{2},…,{c}_{k}\\in R$ is also a solution of the same homogeneous system. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given expression.\n\n- Option I is a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Indeed, notice that the linear combination \n\n$$\n{x}_{h}(t)=2\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)+1\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)\n$$\n\nis a solution to the homogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ according to the linearity principle. Thus, \n\n$$\n{{2\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{h}(t)}+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nis a solution to our inhomogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t).$\n\n- Option II might be *not* a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Notice that $-5\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{p}(t)$ can't be written as \n\n$$\n{x}_{h}(t)+{x}_{p}(t),\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{h}(t)$ is a solution of the homogeneous system, because of the multiplier $-1$ next to the particular solution ${x}_{p}(t).$\n\n- Option III might be *not* a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Notice that $3\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)-2\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)+3\\cdot {x}_{p}(t)$ can't be written as \n\n$$\n{x}_{h}(t)+{x}_{p}(t),\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{h}(t)$ is a solution of the homogeneous system, because of the multiplier $3$ next to the particular solution ${x}_{p}(t).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"." 13194139535876 true] [17592186371868 1000 #edb/ref 17592186371869 13194139535876 true] [17592186371868 1000 #edb/ref 17592186371870 13194139535876 true] [17592186371868 1000 #edb/ref 17592186371871 13194139535876 true] [17592186371868 1000 #edb/ref 17592186371872 13194139535876 true] [17592186371868 1000 #edb/ref 17592186371873 13194139535876 true] [17592186371868 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535876 true] [17592186371868 1041 "selection" 13194139535876 true] [17592186371868 1104 #edb/ref 17592186371870 13194139535876 true] [17592186371868 1110 #uuid "c6672b5b-947e-53ca-b7bb-3d3e6ea137a9" 13194139535876 true] [17592186371869 1034 "I and III only" 13194139535876 true] [17592186371869 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535876 true] [17592186371869 1111 #uuid "21960f41-79d3-5875-a45b-9f4d4cac9086" 13194139535876 true] [17592186371870 1034 "I only" 13194139535876 true] [17592186371870 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535876 true] [17592186371870 1111 #uuid "7b6a6ae6-6ef6-5e3f-a845-5893f7226672" 13194139535876 true] [17592186371871 1034 "III only" 13194139535876 true] [17592186371871 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535876 true] [17592186371871 1111 #uuid "1fb4222b-bef5-510b-81f7-7a59ef630c18" 13194139535876 true] [17592186371872 1034 "I, II and III" 13194139535876 true] [17592186371872 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535876 true] [17592186371872 1111 #uuid "bdac7e87-c803-54ed-8765-f1a508589ef0" 13194139535876 true] [17592186371873 1034 "II only" 13194139535876 true] [17592186371873 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535876 true] [17592186371873 1111 #uuid "d28f3fef-088f-5d50-9074-8914cf3ae9aa" 13194139535876 true] [17592186371874 1017 #edb/ref 17592186371875 13194139535876 true] [17592186371874 1089 "q-330302" 13194139535876 true] [17592186371874 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535876 true] [17592186371874 1100 "Let ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ satisfy the homogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=A\\text{ }x(t)$ and ${x}_{p}(t)$ satisfy the inhomogeneous system\n${x}^{′}(t)=A\\text{ }x(t)+f(t).$\nWhich of the following is **always** a solution of the same inhomogeneous system?\n\n- ${x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)$\n- $-3\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)$\n- $7\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)-5\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)$" 13194139535876 true] [17592186371874 1113 #uuid "ad686aec-973d-5f95-9289-1f54c1e48676" 13194139535876 true] [17592186371874 1145 "Recall that if ${x}_{h}(t)$ satisfies a homogeneous linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ and ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution to an inhomogeneous linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t),$ then ${x}_{h}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)$ is also a solution to the same inhomogeneous system.\n\nAdditonally, the *linearity principle* for systems of linear ordinary differential equations (ODEs) states that if ${x}_{1}(t),$ ${x}_{2}(t),…,{x}_{k}(t)$ are solutions to a homogeneous linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ then any linear combination \n\n$$\n{c}_{1}{x}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{x}_{2}(t)+⋯+{c}_{k}{x}_{k}(t)\n$$\n\nwith ${c}_{1},{c}_{2},…,{c}_{k}\\in R$ is also a solution of the same homogeneous system. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given expression.\n\n- Option I is a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Indeed, notice that the linear combination \n\n$$\n{x}_{h}(t)={x}_{1}(t)\n$$\n\nis a solution to the homogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ according to the linearity principle. Thus, \n\n$$\n{{{x}_{1}(t)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{h}(t)}+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nis a solution to our inhomogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t).$\n\n- Option II is a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Indeed, notice that the linear combination \n\n$$\n{x}_{h}(t)=-3\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)\n$$\n\nis a solution to the homogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ according to the linearity principle. Thus, \n\n$$\n{{-3\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{h}(t)}+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nis a solution to our inhomogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t).$\n\n- Option III is a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Indeed, notice that the linear combination \n\n$$\n{x}_{h}(t)=7\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)-5\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)\n$$\n\nis a solution to the homogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ according to the linearity principle. Thus, \n\n$$\n{{7\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)-5\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{h}(t)}+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nis a solution to our inhomogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II and III\"." 13194139535876 true] [17592186371875 1000 #edb/ref 17592186371876 13194139535876 true] [17592186371875 1000 #edb/ref 17592186371877 13194139535876 true] [17592186371875 1000 #edb/ref 17592186371878 13194139535876 true] [17592186371875 1000 #edb/ref 17592186371879 13194139535876 true] [17592186371875 1000 #edb/ref 17592186371880 13194139535876 true] [17592186371875 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535876 true] [17592186371875 1041 "selection" 13194139535876 t